        "id": "ae54ad61-c8ee-4b5e-963c-bb33e8cd5ca6",
        "type": "spacer",
        "order": 3,
        "markdown": ""
      },
      {
        "id": "18ebe3ce-e879-4f44-b1e1-25a3988e1f17",
        "type": "richtext",
        "order": 4,
        "markdown": "### Kafka once said, “To be free, is to be lost.”\nI have always felt the same way. The more choices I gathered for myself, the more outrageously difficult it got for me to find a direction. I had to travel, I wanted to create films, I also wanted a secure financial future, I wanted to be in love, to read and write poetry; and so much more.\n\nBut being away; being lost; traveling without destinations or plans taught me quite differently. In a way that it’s quite on the contrary to what Kafka said; “To be lost by your own actions, is to be free.” as well right? What a luxury it is to be able to feel lost by your own free will.\n\nEven though, things do not often pivot through the edges in your life the way you wished they would, the subtle moments of gratitude take a lot of pressure off your head. Gratitude and patience, will take us a long way. I have learnt it the hard way and I believe it was of utmost importance in my life.\n\nBeing 25, being an adult, doesn’t feel all to big or lost anymore."
      },
      {
        "id": "6df84e2e-1dd1-4802-967c-161e507d95eb",
